>
  <p>
    Follow the quick start top to bottom to get a component rendering. Everything after it
    explains what each step did and the options you can change.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>

  <p><strong>1. Create a Marko app.</strong></p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=QS_CREATE/>

  <p><strong>2. Move into it.</strong></p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=QS_CD/>

  <p>
    <strong>3. Run <code>init</code>.</strong> It sets up Tailwind, the theme, and
    <code>components.json</code> for you. In a terminal it asks for a base color, a distribution,
    and a visual style; press Enter at each question to accept <code>neutral</code>,
    <code>copy</code>, and <code>vega</code>. <code>bunx marko-ui init --defaults</code> skips the
    questions.
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=QS_INIT/>

  <p>To skip the questions and take those defaults, pass <code>--defaults</code>:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=INIT_DEFAULTS/>

  <p><strong>4. Add a component.</strong></p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=QS_ADD/>

  <p>
    <strong>5. Use it.</strong> Replace the contents of the scaffold's
    <code>src/routes/+page.marko</code> with this. If you put it in another file, adjust the
    relative import path to where that file sits:
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" filename="src/routes/+page.marko" code=QS_USE/>

  <p><strong>6. Run the dev server.</strong></p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=QS_DEV/>

  <p>
    Open the URL it prints. You should see a "Hello" heading and a styled outline button labelled
    "Click me". The button is now source in your repo at
    <code>src/components/ui/button/button.marko</code>. Next,
    <a href="#ai-agent">set up your AI agent</a> if you use one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>

  <p>
    Commands use <code>bunx</code> and <code>bun run</code>. With npm, use <code>npx</code> and
    <code>npm run</code>; with pnpm, <code>pnpm dlx</code> and <code>pnpm run</code>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Marko 6.</strong> A fresh <code>create-marko</code> app is one. <code>init</code> needs a <code>package.json</code> that depends on <code>marko</code>, <code>@marko/run</code> or <code>@marko/vite</code> (the scaffold has it). Otherwise, run in a terminal, it offers to install Marko and asks first; non-interactive runs refuse; <code>--force</code> bypasses the check, and at a workspace root it tells you to run from the app or pass <code>--cwd &lt;app&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tailwind v4.</strong> On a fresh <code>create-marko</code> app you do not set it up:
      <code>init</code> installs it and wires it in (see <a href="#what-init-does">what
      <code>init</code> changes</a>). In an existing project, <code>init</code> reuses the
      Tailwind stylesheet it finds, creates one if there is none, and warns when your own Vite
      config lacks <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code>.
      <a href="/docs/components-json">components.json</a> explains how the CLI finds your CSS file
      and path aliases.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>TypeScript ^5 or ^6.</strong> TypeScript 7 (the native <code>tsgo</code>
      compiler) is not supported — it ships no in-process compiler API, which both marko-ui's own
      tooling and the component build depend on. Keep your project on <code>typescript ^5</code> or
      <code>^6</code> until marko-ui's TS 7 support lands.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Node.js 20.18.1 or newer.</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-init-does">What init changes</h2>

  <p>
    On a fresh <code>create-marko</code> app, <code>marko-ui init</code> makes exactly these
    changes:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Writes <code>components.json</code>.</li>
    <li>Adds <code>allowImportingTsExtensions</code> to <code>tsconfig.json</code>.</li>
    <li>Creates <code>src/styles/globals.css</code> and writes the theme into it, inside a <code>/* marko-ui:theme:start */ … /* marko-ui:theme:end */</code> block.</li>
    <li>Adds <code>import "../styles/globals.css"</code> to <code>src/routes/+layout.marko</code>.</li>
    <li>Creates <code>vite.config.ts</code> loading <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code>.</li>
    <li>Installs <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code>, <code>tailwindcss</code>, and <code>tw-animate-css</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <code>marko-ui add button</code> then writes <code>src/components/ui/button/button.marko</code>
    and <code>src/lib/utils.ts</code>, and installs the npm packages that component needs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="init">marko-ui init</h2>

  <p>
    The theme always lands inside a <code>/* marko-ui:theme:start */ … /* marko-ui:theme:end */</code>
    block in the <code>tailwind.css</code> file named in <code>components.json</code>. A re-run
    replaces only that block. In a stylesheet that holds your own CSS the block is appended; a
    fresh app gets <code>src/styles/globals.css</code> created with it.
  </p>

  <p>
    In an interactive terminal, <code>init</code> with no flags asks three questions: base color,
    distribution, and visual style. Non-interactive callers — an AI agent's shell, CI, piped
    stdin — and <code>--defaults</code> skip the questions and get base color
    <code>neutral</code>, distribution <code>copy</code>, and visual style <code>vega</code>. It
    prints <code>Non-interactive run — using base color neutral, distribution copy, visual style vega.</code>
    so you can see what it chose.
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=INIT_DEFAULTS/>

  <p>To skip the questions and choose yourself, pass the flags:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" filename="copy distribution" code=INIT_COPY/>
  <code-block language="bash" filename="import distribution" code=INIT_IMPORT/>

  <p>
    <code>--distribution</code> accepts <code>copy</code> (the default) or <code>import</code>.
    <code>--visual-style</code> accepts <code>rhea</code>, <code>nova</code>, <code>vega</code>
    (the default), <code>lyra</code>, <code>maia</code>, <code>mira</code>, <code>luma</code>,
    <code>sera</code>, or <code>new-york</code> — see <a href="/docs/theming">Theming</a> for what each one changes. Both
    flags are recorded in <code>components.json</code> so later <code>add</code> and
    <code>eject</code> calls know which mode and style you're on. The full flag list is in the
    <a href="/docs/cli">CLI reference</a>.
  </p>

  <p>On <code>copy</code>, <code>init</code> writes <code>components.json</code>, then fetches and writes the theme matching your base color plus any components you named on the command line, as flat generated source under your components alias directory.</p>

  <p>On <code>import</code>, <code>init</code> writes <code>components.json</code>, adds <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code> as a dependency, and scaffolds the CSS entry described below. No component files are written — there's nothing to copy.</p>

  <h2 id="add-components">Adding components</h2>

  <p>On the <code>copy</code> distribution, install any component by name:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=ADD_COMMAND/>

  <p>Several at once:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=ADD_MANY/>

  <p>
    The CLI resolves each item's registry dependencies, installs the <code>@zag-js/*</code>
    packages that component needs, and writes the <code>.marko</code> files under your components
    directory. Then import the tag by path and use it:
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" code=USE_BUTTON_COPY/>

  <p>
    On the <code>import</code> distribution there is no <code>add</code> step (<code>marko-ui add</code>
    exits with a message on this distribution) — every component is
    already available from <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code>:
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" code=USE_BUTTON_IMPORT/>

  <p>
    Every component's page in the <a href="/docs/components">Components</a> section shows the
    exact install command and a live demo.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-agent">Set up your AI agent</h2>

  <p>
    In a new project, add <code>--agents</code> to <code>init</code> to set up the project and your
    agent in one step. In an initialized project, run the same command (it skips project setup) or
    <code>agents sync</code>:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=INIT_AGENTS/>
  <code-block language="bash" code=AGENTS_SYNC/>

  <p>
    This writes a <code>marko-ui</code> section into <code>AGENTS.md</code> at the project root
    (your own content outside it is kept) and installs the <code>marko-ui</code> and
    <code>marko6</code> agent skills (plus <code>marko-run</code> in an <code>@marko/run</code>
    project, which the scaffold is) into <code>.agents/skills/</code> through the
    <a href="https://skills.sh" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">skills</a> package. Commit both.
    Claude Code reads <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, not <code>AGENTS.md</code>, so add the line
    <code>@AGENTS.md</code> to your <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.
  </p>

  <p>Without the marko-ui CLI, or if the skill install failed, install the skills by hand:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=AGENTS_MANUAL/>

  <p>
    The full guide — what gets written, the CLI commands agents use, and how to check your setup —
    is <a href="/docs/working-with-ai">Working with AI</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="existing-projects">Existing projects</h2>

  <p>
    Running <code>init</code> in a project that already has a <code>components.json</code> fails:
  </p>

  <code-block language="text" code=ALREADY_INIT_ERROR/>

  <p>Run the command that matches what you want instead:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>marko-ui add &lt;name&gt;</code> to add components.</li>
    <li><code>marko-ui agents sync</code> to set up or refresh the AI agent files.</li>
    <li><code>marko-ui init --force</code> to start over. It overwrites the existing configuration.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    To adopt marko-ui in a project that has no <code>components.json</code> yet, run
    <code>init</code> there. It reuses the Tailwind stylesheet it finds, creates one if there is none,
    and warns when your own Vite config lacks <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code>; see
    <a href="/docs/components-json">components.json</a> for how the CLI finds the stylesheet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choosing">Choosing a distribution</h2>

  <p>
    marko-ui ships components two different ways. The quick start used the default, copy. Pick
    the one that matches what you want to own:
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>copy</strong> (<code>marko-ui add</code>) writes flat generated component source
    straight into your project — Tailwind utility classes baked in, no runtime dependency on
    marko-ui. This is the shadcn model: once it's in your repo, the code is yours to edit however
    you like.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>import</strong> installs <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code>, a single npm package that ships
    the same components as <code>mu-*</code> hook-class components plus all 9 visual
    style layers. Nothing is copied into your project; you theme, override, or switch between
    styles entirely from your own stylesheet. Pick this when you expect to swap styles at runtime
    or theme centrally without maintaining a fork of the component source.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import-css">The import CSS entry</h2>

  <p>
    <code>marko-ui init --distribution import</code> writes this block into your Tailwind
    stylesheet automatically. If you're wiring it up by hand, or checking what got generated, it
    has to look exactly like this:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" code=IMPORT_CSS_ENTRY/>

  <p>
    Two parts of this are easy to get wrong, and both fail silently — everything still renders, so
    the break only shows up later as "my override doesn't do anything."
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong><code>layer(components)</code> is required, not stylistic.</strong> Imported raw, a
    style layer's rules land outside any cascade layer, and unlayered CSS beats every layered
    declaration unconditionally — including Tailwind's own <code>@layer utilities</code>. Writing
    <code>class="rounded-none"</code> on a marko-ui <code>Button</code> silently loses to the style
    layer no matter how specific your selector is, if the layer was imported without
    <code>layer(components)</code>:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" filename="don't do this" code=IMPORT_CSS_BROKEN/>

  <p>
    <strong>The <code>@custom-variant</code> line must be present.</strong> Components rely on
    Tailwind variants like <code>data-open:</code> and <code>data-state-*:</code>, which only exist
    because <code>globals.css</code> in the copy path defines them via <code>@custom-variant</code>.
    The import path doesn't get that for free — without the line above in your own build, those
    variant classes parse fine and compile to nothing. No error, no warning, just dead styling.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you picked a base color other than neutral at <code>init</code> time, the generated import
    points at <code>globals-&lt;color&gt;.css</code> instead of <code>globals.css</code> (for
    example <code>globals-zinc.css</code>), and the style layer and custom variant both use your
    chosen <code>--visual-style</code> in place of <code>vega</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="eject">marko-ui eject</h2>

  <p>
    <code>eject</code> switches a project from <code>import</code> to <code>copy</code>. It is not
    an escape hatch bolted on after the fact — under dual distribution it's a first-class,
    supported transition for when you outgrow "theme from CSS" and want to edit component source
    directly.
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=EJECT_COMMAND/>

  <p>
    It reads which components are actually in use from <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code>'s installed
    <code>ui/</code> tree, cross-checks that list against the registry, then fetches and writes
    local source for every one of them — the exact same fetch-and-write path <code>add</code> uses,
    so the result is indistinguishable from having run <code>add</code> for each component by hand.
    It then flips <code>components.json</code>'s <code>distribution</code> field to
    <code>copy</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>eject</code> does not remove the <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code> npm dependency or the CSS
    block <code>init --distribution import</code> wrote — the command prints both as manual
    follow-up steps, since your project keeps rendering correctly with the old CSS in place until
    you remove it and reinstall the copy-path theme (<code>marko-ui add style</code>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="mu-hooks">mu-* styling hooks</h2>

  <p>
    Every marko-ui component carries semantic <code>mu-*</code> classes in its markup —
    <code>mu-button</code>, <code>mu-accordion-trigger</code>, and so on — regardless of which
    distribution you're on. On <code>copy</code> these are inert decoration; the generated Tailwind
    utility classes sitting alongside them do the actual styling, and editing that file is how you
    change anything.
  </p>

  <p>
    On <code>import</code> they're load-bearing: <code>mu-*</code> is the public styling API a
    consumer's own stylesheet targets. Scope an override to one visual style by combining it with
    the <code>style-&lt;name&gt;</code> class your CSS entry activates:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" code=MU_OVERRIDE_CSS/>

  <p>
    Switching styles at runtime — no rebuild, no reinstall — is just toggling which
    <code>style-&lt;name&gt;</code> class is present on an ancestor element. All 9
    layers ship in the package at once, so this works the moment you import more than one of them:
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" code=MU_SWITCH_CLASS/>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>

  <h3>"A components.json file already exists"</h3>
  <p>
    <code>init</code> refuses to run twice in the same project. Nothing is wrong: use
    <code>marko-ui add &lt;name&gt;</code> for components or <code>marko-ui agents sync</code> for
    the agent files, and <code>marko-ui init --force</code> only if you want to start over. See
    <a href="#existing-projects">existing projects</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>My agent does not use the marko-ui CLI, or the skill is missing</h3>
  <p>
    Run <code>bunx marko-ui agents sync --check</code>. It exits 0 when the setup is current and
    3 when <code>AGENTS.md</code> is stale or a skill is missing; <code>bunx marko-ui agents sync</code>
    fixes both. If your agent is Claude Code, confirm <code>CLAUDE.md</code> contains
    <code>@AGENTS.md</code>. More in
    <a href="/docs/working-with-ai#troubleshooting">Working with AI</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>An override or a data-* variant class does nothing</h3>
  <p>
    On the import distribution this is almost always the CSS entry: either the style layer was
    imported without <code>layer(components)</code>, or the matching
    <code>@custom-variant style-&lt;name&gt;</code> line is missing. Both fail silently — see
    <a href="#import-css">the import CSS entry</a> above.
  </p>

  <h3>Components render unstyled</h3>
  <p>
    On the copy distribution, the theme stylesheet is usually not loading: check that the file
    recorded as <code>tailwind.css</code> in <code>components.json</code> is imported by your root
    layout and that <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code> is in your Vite config. <code>init</code> wires
    both. Only if your components live outside the directory Tailwind scans does it also need an
    <code>@source</code> directive for that directory.
  </p>

  <h3>"Cannot find module" for a @zag-js package</h3>
  <p>
    <code>marko-ui add</code> installs a component's declared dependencies automatically. If you
    copied files manually instead, install them yourself — the list is in the component's registry
    JSON under <code>dependencies</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Serialization errors on interaction</h3>
  <p>
    A Zag service or API object escaped into reactive state. This is a component-authoring mistake
    rather than an installation problem — see <a href="/docs/creating-components">Creating
    Components</a> for the rule and the fix.
  </p>

  <h3>Type errors mentioning tsgo, or "cannot read properties of undefined"</h3>
  <p>
    Your project is on TypeScript 7. marko-ui's tooling and build need the classic TypeScript
    compiler API, which TS 7's native <code>tsgo</code> compiler doesn't ship. Downgrade to
    <code>typescript ^5</code> or <code>^6</code> — run <code>marko-ui doctor</code> to confirm.
  </p>
</docs-article>
